<!--
    regsigned: a register local declared at the width its values are sign
    extended from.

    clang -Os (thumbv7em) of
        int total(const short *p, int n) {
          int s = 0;
          for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {     (no unrolling)
            short v = p[i]; if (v < 0) v = -1; s += v * 3 + (v >> 1);
          }
          return s;
        }
    keeps `v` sign-extended in a whole register:
        1:  ldrsh r3,[r0],#2; subs r1,#1; orr.w r3,r3,r3,asr #31
            add.w r12,r3,r3,lsl #1; add.w r2,r2,r3,asr #1; add r2,r12; bne 1b
    Every value of the web (the `ldrsh`, and the `orr` of it with its own sign)
    is the sign extension of its low two bytes, so with the option on `v` is
    an `int2` and the `(int4)` casts on the load go away (#1, #2).  By default
    (option off) it is `int4` (#3, #4).
    Two controls keep the register's width either way:
        keep:   int v = p[n & 7]; if (v < 0) v = n;  one value of the web is
                the input `n`, which is no sign extension (#5).
        widen:  int v = k ? p[0] : 0;  at -O0 (optnone): the value is stored
                to its 4-byte frame slot (`str r0,[sp]`) before it is read back,
                so the program's own home for it is an `int` (#6).

    Stage: P3 simplification quiescence (fullloop's ActionDeadCode rebuilds the
    web at the narrow width through SubvariableFlow's sign-extended mode).  P0
    assertion: option regsigned on|off.  Pass 1 (default, off) keeps the
    register-wide local; pass 2 (`option regsigned on`) proves the feature; the
    controls are the same in both passes.
-->
